Disk Partitioning and LVM configuration
===============================================================
Goal: Use the new 10 GB disk (sdb): create a GPT partition, make
PV -> VG (dbvg) -> LV (dblv, 5GB), format with XFS, mount on
/var/lib/mysql, make it permanent in /etc/fstab using UUID, verify.

WARNING: Be sure /dev/sdb is the NEW empty disk before you start.
         fdisk will erase whatever is on the disk you choose.


STEP 1: Identify the new disk with lsblk
----------------------------------------
$ lsblk

sdb (10G, no partitions, no mountpoint) is the new disk.
(sda is the OS disk, do not touch it.)


STEP 2: Create GPT partition table and one partition using fdisk
----------------------------------------------------------------
$ sudo fdisk /dev/sdb

Inside fdisk, type these one by one:

  g          -> create a new GPT partition table
  n          -> new partition
  1          -> partition number (press Enter for default)
  Enter      -> first sector (default)
  Enter      -> last sector (default = use whole disk)
  w          -> write changes and exit

(Optional: type "t" then "44" or "lvm" to set type to Linux LVM.
 The practical works without it too.)

Check the partition was created:
$ lsblk
(you should see sdb1 under sdb)


STEP 3: Create PV, VG named 'dbvg', and LV named 'dblv' (5GB)
-------------------------------------------------------------
$ sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb1
$ sudo vgcreate dbvg /dev/sdb1
$ sudo lvcreate -L 5G -n dblv dbvg

Display the LV:
$ sudo lvdisplay
(look for LV Path: /dev/dbvg/dblv, LV Size: 5.00 GiB)

Shorter views:
$ sudo pvs
$ sudo vgs
$ sudo lvs


STEP 4: Format the LV with XFS file system
------------------------------------------
$ sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/dbvg/dblv


STEP 5: Mount to /var/lib/mysql and add to /etc/fstab using UUID
----------------------------------------------------------------
Create the mount point and mount it:
$ sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/mysql
$ sudo mount /dev/dbvg/dblv /var/lib/mysql

Get the UUID of the LV:
$ sudo blkid /dev/mapper/dbvg-dblv
(copy the UUID="...." value)

Edit fstab:
$ sudo nano /etc/fstab

Add this line at the end (replace <UUID> with your real UUID):

UUID=<UUID>  /var/lib/mysql  xfs  defaults  0 0

Save in nano: Ctrl+O, Enter, then Ctrl+X

Reload systemd and test the fstab entry:
$ sudo systemctl daemon-reload
$ sudo mount -av

Expected: /var/lib/mysql : already mounted
(no errors = fstab line is correct)


STEP 6: Verify with lsblk -f and df -h
--------------------------------------
$ lsblk -f
(shows sdb1 -> LVM2_member, dbvg-dblv -> xfs, UUID, /var/lib/mysql)

$ df -h
(shows /dev/mapper/dbvg-dblv  5.0G  ...  /var/lib/mysql)

Optional (shows filesystem type too):
$ df -Th


QUICK COMMAND SUMMARY
---------------------
lsblk                              -> find new disk (sdb)
sudo fdisk /dev/sdb                -> g, n, 1, Enter, Enter, w
sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb1            -> create physical volume
sudo vgcreate dbvg /dev/sdb1       -> create volume group
sudo lvcreate -L 5G -n dblv dbvg   -> create 5GB logical volume
sudo lvdisplay                     -> display LV
sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/dbvg/dblv       -> format as XFS
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/mysql       -> create mount point
sudo mount /dev/dbvg/dblv /var/lib/mysql  -> mount
sudo blkid /dev/mapper/dbvg-dblv   -> get UUID
sudo nano /etc/fstab               -> add UUID line
sudo mount -av                     -> test fstab
lsblk -f                           -> verify
df -h                              -> verify
